Q: Why this code doesn&#39;t generate circles but instead does this? A: <blockquote>
<p>Why this code doesn't generate circles</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Primary issue:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It looks as though you've ended up with a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moir%C3%A9_pattern" rel="nofollow noreferrer">moiré effect</a>. You've calculated the colour as if it were a single channel with 2²⁴ levels, not three channels each with 2⁸ levels. ... <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79865195/why-this-code-doesnt-generate-circles-but-instead-does-this#comment140936926_79865195">@
Weather Vane</a></p>
</blockquote>
<p>To achieve a grey scale, assigned the values [0...0xFF] to each primary.</p>
<pre><code>// Sawtooth grey scale.
data[i*PIXEL_COLS + j]  = (uint32_t) trunc(0xFF * cos_dis) * 0x010101;
</code></pre>
<p>To achieve a rainbow scale, assigned the values [0...0xFF] split over 2 pairs of  primaries.</p>
<pre><code>// Simplifications exists.  Verbose for clarity.
// Other color wheel models exist - the below is just one.
unsigned index  = trunc(0xFF*3 * cos_dis);
unsigned hue = index%0x100;
unsigned pair = index/0x100;
#define PRI3 0
uint32_t r,g,b;
switch(pair) {
  case 0: r = hue; g = PRI3; b = 0xFF-hue; break;
  case 1: r = 0xFF-hue; g = hue; b = PRI3; break;
  case 2: r = PRI3; g = 0xFF-hue; b = hue; break;
}
r &lt;&lt;= 0;
g &lt;&lt;= 8;
b &lt;&lt;= 16;
data[i*PIXEL_COLS + j]  = b | g | r;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>[edit]</p>
<p><code>cos_dis</code> to color needed much re-work.  Some alternate ideas.   See <a href="https://blog.johnnovak.net/2016/09/21/what-every-coder-should-know-about-gamma/#gradients" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Gradients</a> for additional gradient issues.</p>
<pre><code>// Triangle grey scale.
uint32_t index  = (uint32_t) lround(0xFF*2 * cos_dis);
uint32_t grey;
if (index &lt; 0xFF) {
  grey = index;
} else {
  grey = 0xFF - (index%0xFF);  // Simplifications exists.
}
data[i*PIXEL_COLS + j]  = grey * 0x010101;

// Triangle color.
unsigned index  = (uint32_t) lround(0xFF*6 * cos_dis);
unsigned hue = index%0xFF;
unsigned pair = index/0xFF;
#define PRI3 0  /* or 0xFF */
uint32_t r,g,b;
switch(pair) {
  case 0: r = 0xFF;     g = hue;  b = 0; break;
  case 1: r = 0xFF-hue; g = 0xFF; b = 0; break;
  case 2: g = 0xFF;     b = hue;  r = 0; break;
  case 3: g = 0xFF-hue; b = 0xFF; r = 0; break;
  case 4: b = 0xFF;     r = hue;  g = 0; break;
  case 5: b = 0xFF-hue; r = 0xFF; g = 0; break;
}
r &lt;&lt;= 0;
g &lt;&lt;= 8;
b &lt;&lt;= 16;
data[i*PIXEL_COLS + j]  = b | g | r;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Subtle thought.  There is an off-by-one concern.</p>
<p>Let us review a 2-bit grey for simplicity.<br />
Review <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bracket_(mathematics)#Intervals" rel="nofollow noreferrer">brackets</a>.</p>
<p>Say we want to map [0.0 ... 1.0] to 2-bits of grey: 0:black, 1:dkgry, 2:ltgry, 3:white.  Various approach exist.</p>
<pre><code>(int) x*3  // This is what OP was doing, and my first attempt.
[0.00 .. 0.33):black,[0.33 .. 0.67):dkgry,[0.67 .. 1.00):ltgry,[1.00 .. 1.00]:white.

(int) round(x*3) // This answer's alternate via lround().
[0.00 .. 0.17):black,[0.17 .. 0.50):dkgry,[0.50 .. 0.67):ltgry,[0.67 .. 1.00]:white.

(x == 1.0) ? 3 : (int) (x*4) // Another alternative and perhaps best.
[0.00 .. 0.25):black,[0.25 .. 0.50):dkgry,[0.50 .. 0.75):ltgry,[0.75 .. 1.00]:white.
</code></pre>
<p>The last method can often avoid the <code>x == 1.0</code> test as the true range of <code>x</code> is <code>[0.0 ... 1.0)</code> and not <code>[0.0 ... 1.0]</code> - yet beware of of being  overconfident on this point.</p>

Q: Software developer ,Web developer A: <pre><code>// MainActivity.java
package com.jarvisai.app;

import android.os.Bundle;
import android.speech.tts.TextToSpeech;
import android.speech.RecognizerIntent;
import android.content.Intent;
import android.widget.Button;
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Locale;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {

    TextToSpeech tts;
    Button listenButton;
    final int REQ_CODE_SPEECH_INPUT = 100;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

        listenButton = findViewById(R.id.listenButton);

        tts = new TextToSpeech(this, status -&gt; {
            if(status != TextToSpeech.ERROR){
                tts.setLanguage(Locale.US);
            }
        });

        listenButton.setOnClickListener(v -&gt; startVoiceInput());
    }

    private void startVoiceInput() {
        Intent intent = new Intent(RecognizerIntent.ACTION_RECOGNIZE_SPEECH);
        intent.putExtra(RecognizerIntent.EXTRA_LANGUAGE_MODEL, RecognizerIntent.LANGUAGE_MODEL_FREE_FORM);
        intent.putExtra(RecognizerIntent.EXTRA_LANGUAGE, Locale.getDefault());
        intent.putExtra(RecognizerIntent.EXTRA_PROMPT, &quot;Say something...&quot;);
        try {
            startActivityForResult(intent, REQ_CODE_SPEECH_INPUT);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            speak(&quot;Sorry, voice input not supported&quot;);
        }
    }

    @Override
    protected void onActivityResult(int requestCode, int resultCode, Intent data) {
        super.onActivityResult(requestCode, resultCode, data);

        if(requestCode == REQ_CODE_SPEECH_INPUT &amp;&amp; resultCode == RESULT_OK &amp;&amp; data != null){
            ArrayList&lt;String&gt; result = data.getStringArrayListExtra(RecognizerIntent.EXTRA_RESULTS);
            String command = result.get(0).toLowerCase();
            handleCommand(command);
        }
    }

    private void handleCommand(String command){
        switch(command){
            case &quot;hello&quot;:
                speak(&quot;Boss&quot;);
                break;
            case &quot;hay&quot;:
                speak(&quot;Boss&quot;);
                break;
            default:
                speak(&quot;I did not understand, Boss&quot;);
                break;
        }
    }

    private void speak(String text){
        tts.speak(text, TextToSpeech.QUEUE_FLUSH, null, null);
    }
}
</code></pre>
